---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import P from "../components/P.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import d from "../data/the-110.json";
import stats from "../data/stats.json";
import focus from "../data/focus.json";
const M = focus.Mazza.groups[0];
---
<Base title="The Hundred and Ten" eyebrow="Answered, 10 September 2026"
      dek="From the day this archive was built its largest open question was a hundred and ten Piedimonte Etneo Mazza joined to the family by nothing at all. They are Salvatore's brothers' descendants.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">The export this archive was built from was <strong>fourteen disconnected
    pieces</strong>. The largest held the family. The second held <strong>a hundred and ten Mazza of
    Piedimonte Etneo</strong> — a complete kindred with its own marriages, children and cousins —
    headed by a <strong>Rosario Mazza born 1850</strong>, married to a <strong>Leonarda</strong> with
    no surname, and attached to Michael Mazza's line by <em>nothing</em>.</p>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:1rem">Separately, the family tree gave Salvatore Mazza a father
    called <strong>Rosario</strong> about whom one thing was asserted and nothing was known. Two
    Rosario Mazza in one small town. The archive refused to merge them for a day, on the grounds that
    <a href={u("/method")}>a shared name is not evidence</a>.</p>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:1rem"><strong>Five records now say they are one man.</strong></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The argument</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Rosario Mazza and Leonarda Bonanno, and their sons</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin:.9rem 0 1.4rem">An Italian marriage proclamation states the ages of
    <em>both the bride and groom and their parents</em>. Three of them, across twelve years, name the
    same couple and give Rosario's age each time — 53 in 1898, 55 in 1902, 65 in 1910. Two birth acts
    do the same — 28 in 1873, 31 in 1877, 32 in 1880. <strong>One man, born about 1845, married to
    Leonarda Bonanno, of Piedimonte Etneo.</strong></p>

    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Son</th><th class="m">Born, by the records</th><th class="m">The tree says</th><th>Evidence</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{d.children.map((c) => (
          <tr>
            <td><a href={u("/people/" + c.slug)}><strong>{c.name}</strong></a></td>
            <td class="m">{c.born}</td>
            <td class="m">{c.treeBorn || "—"}</td>
            <td>{c.note}</td>
          </tr>))}
          {d.undocumented.map((e) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{e.name}</td><td class="m">{e.born}</td>
              <td class="m" style="color:var(--ink-3)">not in the tree</td>
              <td>{e.note}</td>
            </tr>))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="note" style="margin-top:24px"><strong>The tree's Rosario has three sons — Orazio,
    Ignazio and Giovanni — by a wife called Leonarda.</strong> The register gives Rosario and
    Leonarda Bonanno six sons, and three of them are Orazio, Giovanni and Ignazio. The fourth is
    <P n="Salvatore Mazza" />, who sailed nothing and stayed put, and whose son
    <P n="Sebastiano Mazza" /> took the <em>Toscana</em> to Australia in 1949.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">And the name that seals it</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Mariano</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Salvatore's act of birth calls his father <strong>«Rosario Mazza di Mariano»</strong> — son
      of Mariano. Nothing in the family tree names a Mariano in that generation.</p>
      <p>But the tree's unjoined hundred and ten contains a <strong>Mariano Mazza</strong>, and he is
      a <strong>son of Orazio</strong> — Rosario's son. Under the Sicilian naming custom a man's
      first son takes his own father's father's name, so Orazio naming a boy <em>Mariano</em> is
      exactly what you would expect if Orazio's grandfather was Mariano.</p>
      <p><strong>The custom and the document agree — and this time the document was read twice.</strong>
      Earlier today this archive built the same kind of argument on the name <em>Venerio</em> and had
      to <a href={u("/register-1879")}>withdraw it</a>, because the reading underneath it was wrong.
      «Di Mariano» is written plainly, in a clerk's careful hand, and the Mariano in the tree was
      entered by a relative who had never seen the act.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">What changed in the archive</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Fourteen pieces became ten</h2>
    <div class="grid g3">
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">581</div><div class="l">people now in one connected family, up from 479</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{M.n}</div><div class="l">Mazza in a single descent group, where there were six</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">106</div><div class="l">of the hundred and ten come through Orazio alone</div></div>
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:24px">Every one of the hundred and ten descends from, or married
    into, Rosario Mazza. They are the families of <strong>Michael Rocco Mazza's great-great-uncles</strong>
    — the brothers Salvatore left behind when his son took ship. <a href={u("/mazza")}>The Mazza →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <div class="lab">How the archive made the join</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">A declared argument, not an automatic rule</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>This build merges two records automatically only on evidence found <em>inside</em> the
      export — a shared birth year, or the same family standing around them. That rule was written
      after an earlier version fused four undated Rosario Mazza on the strength of the name alone and
      <a href={u("/method")}>manufactured a pedigree</a>. It would never have made this join, and it
      was right not to.</p>
      <p>So this join is <strong>declared</strong>, in <code>data/documented-joins.tsv</code>, with
      its citation attached, and applied after the automatic rules as an explicit override. The
      difference matters: <strong>an automatic merge is a rule; a documented join is an argument</strong>,
      and an argument has to be published so it can be attacked.</p>
      <blockquote style="margin-top:1rem">{d.join.evidence}</blockquote>
      <p style="margin-top:1rem"><strong>What would overturn it:</strong> Rosario Mazza's own act of
      marriage, or his death act, naming a father who is not Mariano — or a second Rosario Mazza of
      Piedimonte Etneo, born about 1845, married to a second Leonarda Bonanno, with sons of the same
      four names. The archive would rather that possibility were tested than assumed away.
      <a href={u("/open-questions")}>The queue →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
